begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-010','[["Project management versus project leadership","The schedule and reports are well controlled, but the team lacks purpose, stakeholder commitment and confidence during disruption.","Provide strategic leadership by clarifying purpose, building commitment and adapting direction while retaining management controls.","Add more detailed reports and assume commitment will follow.","Abandon planning and controls because leadership replaces management.","Focus only on enforcing task completion.","Management provides planning and control; leadership creates direction, alignment, influence and adaptability. Successful projects need both."],["Strategic alignment and vision","A technically successful project is producing outputs that no longer support the organisation''s current strategy.","Reconfirm strategic value with governance stakeholders and redirect, rejustify or stop the project as appropriate.","Continue unchanged because technical success is the only project objective.","Hide the strategic misalignment until closure.","Ask the team to work faster on the same outputs.","Strategic project leadership keeps delivery aligned with organisational purpose and intended benefits."],["Adaptive leadership","A policy reversal and funding shock invalidate key assumptions, but the leader continues applying the original plan rigidly.","Mobilise stakeholders to reassess assumptions, learn rapidly and adapt priorities and delivery within governance.","Insist on the original plan to demonstrate consistency.","Make uncommunicated changes without stakeholder involvement.","Wait for certainty before taking any action.","Adaptive leadership responds to complex, changing conditions through learning, engagement and responsible adjustment."],["Tuckman team development","A newly formed team is polite but uncertain about goals, roles and working norms.","Provide structure, clarify purpose and roles, and help the team establish working agreements appropriate to the forming stage.","Assume the team is already performing and withdraw all guidance.","Create competition and conflict to accelerate development.","Evaluate members only on final results before roles are clear.","During forming, teams need orientation, clarity, inclusion and structure before they can mature."],["Managing the storming stage","Team members openly challenge priorities, compete for influence and disagree about how work should be done.","Facilitate constructive conflict, clarify decision rules and refocus the team on shared goals and norms.","Suppress all disagreement and punish anyone who raises concerns.","Ignore the conflict because it will always resolve itself.","Replace the entire team at the first sign of disagreement.","Storming requires active facilitation, role and norm clarification, and constructive conflict management."],["Psychological safety","A junior engineer notices a serious design risk but remains silent because previous messengers were blamed.","Create a safe environment for speaking up, thank the engineer, examine the evidence and address the risk without retaliation.","Require concerns to remain within senior management.","Criticise the engineer for questioning an approved design.","Delay discussion until the defect occurs.","Psychological safety enables early learning, risk detection and honest collaboration without removing accountability."],["Team accountability and RACI","Several people are responsible for an approval, but no single person is accountable and decisions repeatedly stall.","Clarify roles with one accountable owner and appropriate responsible, consulted and informed parties.","Make everyone accountable so no one feels excluded.","Remove all consultation to speed decisions.","Let the team decide accountability after each deadline is missed.","Clear accountability and role interfaces reduce duplication, gaps and decision delay."],["Motivation and high performance","The leader relies only on financial rewards, while team members report low trust, unclear goals and little recognition or autonomy.","Address the broader performance system, including purpose, clarity, capability, trust, feedback, recognition and appropriate autonomy.","Increase pressure and public criticism.","Offer the same reward regardless of contribution or team needs.","Assume motivation is entirely a personal responsibility.","High performance depends on meaningful goals, enabling conditions, trust, competence, feedback and fair recognition, not money alone."],["Sustaining high-performing teams","A team delivered one successful milestone but is now experiencing burnout, knowledge concentration and declining collaboration.","Rebalance workload, protect sustainable pace, share knowledge and reinforce team learning and wellbeing.","Maintain the same pressure because past success proves the pace is sustainable.","Reward only the few people holding critical knowledge.","Postpone wellbeing concerns until project closure.","Sustained performance requires wellbeing, resilience, knowledge sharing, learning and manageable workload."],["Conflict resolution","Two specialists disagree strongly about a technical approach, and the disagreement is becoming personal.","Separate people from the problem, clarify interests and evidence, facilitate options and agree a decision process.","Choose the more senior specialist without examining evidence.","Allow personal attacks so the strongest idea emerges.","Avoid the topic and let both approaches proceed independently.","Constructive conflict management focuses on facts, interests, options and agreed decision criteria while preserving relationships."],["Sustainable leadership and SDG alignment","A project can meet its short-term output target by creating environmental harm and excluding vulnerable users.","Reassess the approach against long-term value, ethics, stakeholder impact and relevant sustainability commitments.","Proceed because scope and schedule override sustainability.","Hide the external impacts from the sponsor.","Transfer all responsibility to the future operations team.","Sustainable leadership considers enduring social, environmental and economic value, not only immediate outputs."],["Emotional intelligence and self-management","A leader becomes defensive during a difficult review and responds angrily before understanding the evidence.","Pause, recognise and regulate the emotional response, then examine the facts and respond constructively.","Use anger to demonstrate authority.","End the meeting and refuse future feedback.","Blame the team before reviewing the information.","Self-awareness and self-regulation help leaders manage emotions and make sound decisions under pressure."],["Empathy and stakeholder communication","A community representative expresses concern in an indirect manner, and the project leader dismisses it because it is not presented in technical language.","Listen actively, explore the underlying concern and adapt communication while maintaining clear project boundaries.","Require all stakeholders to use project-management terminology.","Assume indirect communication means the concern is unimportant.","Promise every requested change to show empathy.","Empathy involves understanding perspectives and adapting communication, while still exercising judgement and governance."],["Constructive feedback and difficult conversations","A high-performing specialist repeatedly undermines colleagues, but the leader avoids the conversation because the person is technically valuable.","Address the behaviour promptly with specific evidence, impact, expected standards and a fair improvement plan.","Ignore the behaviour as long as technical targets are met.","Criticise the specialist publicly without prior discussion.","Transfer affected colleagues instead of addressing the conduct.","Effective feedback is timely, specific, respectful and focused on behaviour, impact and agreed improvement."],["Leading in a VUCA environment","Volatility, uncertainty, complexity and ambiguity produce conflicting information and rapid change across the project.","Use sensemaking, scenario thinking, short feedback cycles, distributed expertise and transparent decisions.","Centralise every decision and wait for complete information.","Commit to a single forecast and ignore emerging evidence.","Reduce communication so uncertainty does not spread.","VUCA conditions require adaptive learning, diverse insight, rapid feedback and clear decision principles."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
